 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the haze in Singapore is still quite bad**, though conditions have eased slightly compared to the severe spike. \n\nSingapore is experiencing its longest continuous spell of unhealthy air quality since the 2015 haze crisis, driven by smoke drifting over from wildfires in Indonesia (primarily Kalimantan).\n\nDetails of the air quality situation include:\n\n* **24-Hour PSI (Pollutant Standards Index):** Air quality remains in the **\"Unhealthy\" range** across all regions of the island. As of late morning, the 24-hour PSI ranged from **139 to 184** (highest in Central Singapore). While this is an improvement from the peak of 229 (\"Very Unhealthy\"), the National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasts that the 24-hour PSI could still fluctuate between **150 and 230** (Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy).\n* **1-Hour PM2.5 Readings (Immediate Air Quality):** As of late morning, PM2.5 concentrations climbed to **158 µg/m³ in Western Singapore** (putting it in the **\"High\"** band), while the rest of the island ranged between **83 and 144 µg/m³** (in the **\"Elevated\"** band). \n* **Closures & Advisories:** Outdoor and non-air-conditioned ActiveSG sports facilities remain temporarily closed. Authorities advise residents—especially the elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions—to minimize or avoid prolonged and strenuous outdoor activities. \n\n**Outlook:**\nThe NEA noted that shifting winds and forecasted showers over Singapore and Kalimantan could bring some gradual relief, though Environment Minister Grace Fu warned that the broader haze situation remains fluid and could persist for weeks. \n\nIf you need to head outdoors, it is best to check the live 1-hour PM2.5 readings on the [NEA website](https://www.nea.gov.sg) or the [Haze Microsite](https://www.haze.gov.sg) right before you leave, and consider wearing an N95 mask if you have to be outside for an extended period.",
